# {{PROJECT_NAME}} - agent instructions

## 1. Workflow

- Never work on `{{MAIN_BRANCH}}`. Each task gets a branch from
  `{{MAIN_BRANCH}}`, named `{{BRANCH_PREFIX}}/<task-id>-<slug>`. (P)
- Push your branch to `origin`. The push is not optional: the reviewer
  verifies from `origin`, not from your report. (P)
- You do not open pull requests and you do not merge. {{OWNER}} merges,
  after {{VERIFIER}} has verified the branch from `origin`. (P)
- Never force-push, never rebase published history, never `git add -A`.
  Stage by explicit path. (P)
- Never stage: {{NEVER_STAGE_PATHS}}. (P)
- Start every task with a sync and a guard: fetch, confirm you are on the
  right base (`git merge-base` / a grep for something the base must
  contain), and record the current baselines (lint count, test count). (P)
- {{OWNER}} is not the first QA tester. A feature is done when a user can
  reach it from the UI, not when its endpoint answers. (P)

## 2. Project context

{{ONE_PARAGRAPH: what the system is, who uses it, primary language}}

Authoritative documents, in order of precedence:
1. `{{SOURCE_OF_TRUTH_DOC}}` - what the system must be.
2. `{{INVARIANTS_DOC}}` - the security floor (section 5).
3. Where docs and code disagree, the code is the truth. Report the
   drift; do not silently follow either side. (P)

## 3. Conventions

- {{BACKEND_CONVENTIONS}}
- {{FRONTEND_CONVENTIONS}}
- Lint baseline is **{{LINT_BASELINE}}**. Add no new violations. When the
  count changes, update it here and in `{{CHECKLIST_DOC}}` in the same
  commit. (P)
- A list rendered from a server collection is always bounded. (P)
- One fact, one place. A second copy of a fact (a duplicated array, a
  hand-kept list beside a derived one) will drift. Derive it instead. (P)
- When a file grows past {{FILE_SIZE_LIMIT}}, add a new, app-scoped file
  rather than growing it further. (P)
- Destructive schema changes (drop, rename, type change) need explicit
  sign-off from {{OWNER}} in the task brief. Migrations are additive and
  backward compatible by default. (P)

## 4. Parallel work

- Several agents may run at once, each in its own git worktree. (P)
- Each brief lists the files the agent owns. Do not edit a file outside
  that list; if you must, stop and report why. (P)
- Agents in the same wave own disjoint files, so `git pull --rebase`
  before pushing is clean. If a push is rejected, pull-rebase and retry. (P)
- Waves are sequential when a later task builds on an earlier one's files. (P)

## 5. Hard invariants

These are listed in `{{INVARIANTS_DOC}}`. Each has an ID, the place it is
enforced and the test that locks it. Any change that contradicts one is a
P0 regression. (P)

- Never weaken, skip or delete an invariant's test to make a change pass.
- A new invariant is added together with its test, in the same commit.
- Paired rules: when a rule has two halves, both ship together. Example
  from the original project: "new tracked field on an audited model ->
  add it to the tracked-fields list AND add a test in the audit suite". (P)

## 6. Definition of done

A task is done when all of these hold, and the report shows each one:

- [ ] Every acceptance item in the brief is met, or listed as NOT done
      with a reason. (P)
- [ ] Tests added or changed for the behaviour; invariant tests untouched
      or strengthened. (P)
- [ ] `{{FAST_CHECKS}}` clean (type check, lint at or below baseline,
      migrations check). (P)
- [ ] The full test suite has run at the close of the task:
      `{{FULL_SUITE_COMMAND}}`. A red is a task item, not a note. (P)
- [ ] Any document that quotes UI text was checked against the running
      screen, not the source bundle. (P)
- [ ] CI result read for the pushed commit. (P)
- [ ] `{{CHECKLIST_DOC}}` updated (NOW / NEXT / SHIPPED) in the same
      branch, not in a later docs-only pass. (P)
- [ ] Adversarial self-review done: what would a reviewer try to break? (P)

## 7. Fix loops

- At most two fix iterations per failing item. If it still fails, report
  it as open with the evidence. Do not keep retrying. (P)
- No unattended agent-versus-agent loops. (P)

## 8. Reporting

End every task with a report that contains:
- branch name and tip commit;
- per acceptance item: done / not done, with evidence (test name, command
  output, screenshot);
- numbers measured, not assumed (test counts, timings, lint count);
- "Deliberately NOT done" and "Where the brief was wrong", if any. (P)
